  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 350 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong to severe thunderstorms are expected \nthis this afternoon and evening, along with the potential for \nlocalized areas of flash flooding through tonight.\n\nShowers/tstms have become more widespread across central VA \nthis aftn well in advance of a cold front and potent upper \ntrough. Deep layer shear has increased to 30-40kt and combined \nwith 2500-3000 J/kg of MLCAPE has resulted in quickly developing\nupdrafts. Instability is expected to remain high through the \nevening while the mid-level flow increases to 40-50kt. \nTherefore, a mix is line segments and semi-discrete cells remain\npossible through the evening. The main threat is damaging wind \ngusts. However, there is a risk for tornadoes (especially N and \nNE where SRH will be higher), and some initial cells late this \naftn could contain large hail. A Severe Thunderstorm Watch is in\neffect through 10 PM this evening.\n\nFast storm motion will initially preclude significant flooding \nconcerns. However, as the front slows this evening into tonight \n(and eventually Wednesday/Wednesday night) the flood threat will\nincrease due to persistent rounds of showers/tstms. There is \npresently an axis of 2.0-2.3\" of PW across central VA to the \nEastern Shore, and this will linger overnight into early \nWednesday morning. An initial Flood Watch has been issued from \ncentral VA to the lower Eastern Shore covering the threat \ntonight into Wednesday. This will eventually need to be expanded\nSE to cover the later Wednesday/Thursday time period.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The threat of severe thunderstorms will linger \ninto Wednesday and the potential for torrential rainfall to \nproduce scattered instances of flash flooding will also increase\nthrough Wednesday night and Thursday.\n\nThe severe threat likely continues into Wednesday, but with the\nhigher probabilities shifted to the south and east ahead of the\naforementioned southward-moving cold front. How convection \nevolves today/tonight will also influence the degree of cloud \ncover and destabilization potential on Wednesday. That said, the\nflow aloft is forecast to remain rather strong (40-50kt at \n500mb). SPC has the Slight Risk outlined for our entire CWA (lvl\n2/5).\n\nDespite the ongoing drought conditions, Wednesday into \nWednesday night is showing an increasingly confident signal for \nlocalized flooding, especially in urban and poor drainage areas.\nWPC has most of the area in a Slight Risk ERO again Wednesday \nwith PW generally 2- 2.4\", and a small Moderate Risk area will \nbe issued for SE VA into NE NC. Additionally, a small Moderate \nRisk area will be introduced including NE NC Thursday. Decent \nflow aloft should keep individual cells moving but training \nstorms on top of the rainfall received today/tonight could cause\nscattered instances of flash flooding. A Flood Watch will \neventually be needed for SE VA and NE NC based on the latest \ndata. All total, the QPF forecast through Thursday has generally\n3-5\", with locally higher amounts of rain from Richmond and \nEmporia eastward to SE MD and NE NC.\n\nSome drier air finally moves back into northern portions of the\nregion Thursday and Friday but some showers and storms will \nremain possible, mainly across the southern and southeastern \nportions of the area. Trending drier with below average \ntemperatures by the weekend.",
